\title{RV-2B: adversarial review of the exact-let mode (package MO-2), from the outside: mutants of the real units, generated exports, the schedule}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{soundness}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Review "RV-2B" of 2026-11-25 (02:49 to about 04:30 UTC of the following UTC day). Its subject is the build of [package MO-2](rep-MO-2): a declaration whose first run reaches a K comparison on a major premise that may be typed through a let variable is checked again in an \em{exact-let mode}, and the machine decides it instead of handing it to the reference engine. The reviewer did not write the change and was asked to find inputs on which it is wrong, as a black box: inputs first, verdicts compared, the official checker as the only judge. A second reviewer read the patch against the official source ([its report](rep-RV-2A)); the two reviews did not share inputs. Nothing was repaired, and nothing of this review is in the main tree.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Result in one page}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Verdict: no-go for the patch entering the default configuration as built.} On 12,621 inputs with an official verdict (12,619 of them; two real mutants ended at the memory limit of the official run) a plain run of the candidate gives another verdict than the official checker on 980, and \strong{245 of these are its own}: on them the published build gives the official verdict, because its machine declines and the reference engine decides. 142 of the 245 are \strong{rejections of exports that the official checker accepts}, 103 are accepts of official rejects. One cause covers all 245 (finding RV2B-F1): inside the mode, a major premise that is a reducible term around the application of a let variable is typed by its reduct.}
\li{\strong{No accept of a statement that is not derivable}, by any build, engine or option, on any input. 1,064 inputs state something that is not derivable (the K type at an opaque index, with let variables of firing types around the one the major premise goes through); every one of them is rejected by the candidate, by its machine alone or after a decline, by the base and by the published build. Every accept against the official verdict in this review is of a derivable statement (the family argument of section 3).}
\li{\strong{What the candidate does better than the published build}, on the same inputs: 11,639 official verdicts in a plain run against 11,002; 558 rejections of official accepts against 678; 422 accepts of official rejects against 939. Its machine alone declines 1,804 inputs where the base's machine declines 4,911. On the shapes the package names as its class (a let variable, its application, a projection of it as the major premise; chains of let variables; lambda bodies; the same comparison inside and after the let; several declarations with one let) a plain run of the candidate gives the official verdict on every input of this review but those of finding RV2B-F2 and one of RV2B-F5.}
\li{\strong{Five more findings are wrong rejects of the published default and of the candidate alike}, none of them repaired or declined by the mode, which is not entered: a trigger gap in the package's own class when only one of the two types of the let variable has the K type at its head (RV2B-F2); the projection of a constructor application as the major premise (RV2B-F3); a redex or let term whose body is the let variable (RV2B-F4); more than 64 alias lets (RV2B-F5); structure eta in iota and the unit-like rule on a stuck projection after a let (RV2B-F6). Findings F3 and F4 were found independently by [the review from the source](rep-RV-2A) (its findings F6 and F5).}
\li{\strong{The real units.} 75 distinct declarations make up the 86 units of the class. Each was cut into an export of its own. The candidate's machine decides 69 of them and declines the six that [the report of the package](rep-MO-2) names; a plain run accepts all 75. On 1,311 structural mutants of them (lets turned into redexes and back, lets inlined, declared types and values wrapped, the major premise of every K recursor wrapped or hidden behind an axiom) a plain run of the candidate gives the official verdict on all 1,309 that have one (1,184 accept, 125 reject), and no mutant turns a decided unit into a declined one. On 654 mutants with a hostile K site grafted into the real declaration it differs on 52, all with a reducible major premise: 46 of them its own (RV2B-F1 inside a real unit).}
\li{\strong{The schedule.} 1,519 files with several declarations that share one let and 492 real mutants, each under 1, 4 and 8 workers on the executable and 1 and 8 on the JVM launcher: no verdict depends on the schedule. The executable and the JVM launcher agree on 3,039 single-declaration exports, in a plain run and for the machine alone. Whether a real unit is answered by the first run or by the mode does depend on the schedule (section 7); the verdict does not.}
\li{\strong{The reference engine} gives the official verdict on all 12,619 inputs.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. Conditions}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Candidate}: the final build of package MO-2 (jars \code{0397eb07054a}, the jar of \code{rlvm-machine} \code{6db085f6b0a5}), the executable; the JVM launcher where it is named. \strong{Base}: the private build of the unpatched tree of 2026-11-25 22:44 UTC that the report of the package names as its second base. \strong{Published build}: a private copy of the shared distribution taken at the start of the review (jars \code{d867a0766427}). Base and published build give the same verdict on every input of this review; "published" below stands for both.}
\li{\strong{Plain run}: no option; a rejection of the machine is final, a decline goes to the reference engine. \strong{Machine alone}: \code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~}; a decline of the machine is exit status 2. \strong{Reference engine}: \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~}. Every run of the executable in the harness had a heap of 3 GiB. Exit status 0 is accept, 1 reject, 2 decline. The reference engine ended with status 3 (out of memory under the 3 GiB of the harness) on the two real mutants that have no official verdict; no other run ended with another status.}
\li{\strong{Official verdict}: \code{\verb~|leanchecker --from-export~} of Lean 4.36.0 (\code{\verb~|lean --version~} in the container: commit \code{611c117bfaf7}), in the project's Docker image without network, 6 GiB of address space and 120 s per export (600 s for the real mutants). Two real mutants (a let of one navier-stokes-euler unit turned into a beta-redex) have no official verdict: the official checker ran out of 6 GiB and, run again, out of 24 GiB of address space. The machine of every build rejects both at once (a let whose value no longer has the declared type); they are counted as inputs and in no other column.}
\li{\strong{The harness} runs, per input: the candidate in a plain run with its statistics file (the counters \code{\verb~|machine.let.*~}), the candidate's machine alone, the candidate with the reference engine, the base in a plain run and its machine alone, the published build in a plain run; for files with several declarations also the candidate under \code{\verb~|--threads 1|4|8~} and the JVM launcher under 1 and 8 workers, and \code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~} on one family. \strong{It sees a fault}: on the seven exports of the two shapes that the package left unrepaired (findings MO2-F3 and MO2-F6) it reports four rejections of official accepts and one accept of an official reject in the candidate and in the published build, the engine disagreement under the differential engine, and agreement on the two controls, as the package's own tables say.}
\li{Host: the aarch64 development host; every bulk run behind \src{bench/slot.sh}, off the measurement cores. Nothing in this report is a timing. Two runs were above 8 GB resident, announced on the board: the official checker on the two mutants just named. The slicer on the Mathlib export needed 3.8 GB.}
}
}

