}

\p{Not generated: a K type in a mutual or nested block. Such a type is not a K target for the official kernel and eliminates into \code{Prop} only, so no comparison of a recursor application with a numeral can be stated; the admission of its recursor is another package's subject. A structure-like proposition with fields (\code{And}) is the environment of every export: \code{\verb~|d h~} is \code{And.rec} on an axiom, which must stay stuck, and does in every build.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. Findings}

\p{Every export named below is one theorem over the triple (240 to 310 lines, most of them the cone of \code{Nat}, \code{And} and \code{Eq}); its official verdict file, the exit status of every build and launcher and the generator are handed over with the review. Kinds: a rejection of an official accept is a \em{wrong reject}; an accept of an official reject is of kind T here (the statement is derivable).}

\subtree{
\title{RV2B-F1: inside the mode, a reducible major premise around the application of a let variable is typed by its reduct (wrong rejects of the candidate only)}

\p{Reported on the board at 03:07 UTC and, wider, at 03:23 UTC. Smallest export, \code{\verb~|rv2b-g1-eq-fn-eta-DF-inW~} (249 lines): \code{\verb~|W : forall (f : Unit -> d intro = d intro), P (Eq.rec 7 ((fun (u : Unit) => f u) Unit.unit))~}, \code{\verb~|ax : 5 = d h~}, and the theorem \code{\verb~|True := let f : Unit -> d intro = d intro := fun u => ax; (fun (_ : P 7) => True.intro) (W f)~}. Official: \strong{accept}; inside the let the major premise is a beta-redex whose body \code{\verb~|f u~} has the declared type of \code{f}, and the K rule fires. Candidate: \strong{reject}, exit status 1 on the executable and on the JVM launcher, \code{APP_TYPE_MISMATCH}, with \code{\verb~|let.units=1~} and \code{\verb~|let.kDual=0~}: a verdict of the mode, made without a comparison of the scope states. Base and published build: accept (their machine declines, the reference engine decides). The candidate with \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.letexact=off~}: accept. Under the differential engine: an engine disagreement.}

\p{\strong{The shapes.} With \code{f} a let variable that is a function and \code{T} a type written in the term: \code{\verb~|(fun (u : Unit) => f u) a~}; \code{\verb~|(fun (g : Unit -> T) => g a) f~}; \code{\verb~|(fun (z : T) => z) (f a)~}; \code{\verb~|(let w : T := f a; w)~}; \code{\verb~|(let w : Unit -> T := f; w a)~}; \code{\verb~|(let w : Unit := a; f w)~}; \code{\verb~|(And.intro T True (f a) True.intro).1~}; \code{\verb~|(fun (g : Unit -> T) (u : Unit) => g u) f a~}; the same with an axiom as the value of \code{f}. Officially the type of each is the binder's domain, the declared type of the inner let, the parameter of the constructor, or the declared type of \code{f} under the binder; the candidate answers by the type of the value of \code{f}. The base flags the application of \code{f}, declines the unit at the K comparison, and the reference engine gives the official verdict; the candidate runs the unit in the mode instead, and the mode types a major premise that has no stored type and is not itself a let variable, a kept application or a projection of one by forcing it. It is the defect of findings MO2-F3 and MO2-F6 (and of RV2B-F3), which the package handed over as unrepaired and outside the mode; this finding is that the mode \em{inherits} it and so turns a decline into a verdict. Where INFER checked the same term inside the let, the type it stored is used and the verdict is the official one: the wrong verdicts are in the positions where the term comes from the type of \code{W} by instantiation, inside the let and after it.}

\p{\strong{The answer also crosses the scope states.} \code{\verb~|rv2b-g8-fn-letunitapp-VF-out.in~}: \code{\verb~|And.intro (let f : Unit -> d h = 5 := fun u => r; W f) (let f : Unit -> d h = 5 := fun u => r; needs7 (Eq.rec 7 (let w : Unit := Unit.unit; f w)))~} with \code{\verb~|r : d intro = 5~}. Official: reject (inside the second let the K rule does not fire). Candidate: \strong{accept}, with \code{\verb~|let.kDual=0~}: the answer made after the first let, by the type of the value, is used inside the second. The statement is derivable, so this is of kind T; it is the claim of section 7.2 of the report of the package that does not hold for these shapes.}

\p{\strong{Counts.} In the family written for these shapes (672 exports): 62 wrong rejects and 64 accepts of official rejects that the published build does not have; its machine declines 433 of the 672, the candidate's 45. Over all sources: 142 wrong rejects and 103 accepts of official rejects of the candidate's own, every one with a reducible major premise around a let variable or its application (families g1, g4, g8, g9: 199; the grafts into real units of section 6: 46). In 12 of the 245 the term is the plain identity redex over the let variable itself (finding MO2-F3), which the published build rejects when it stands alone: there the published build gives the official verdict because the unit is declined for another let application of the same declaration (10, inside real units) or by the order of two declarations (2, section 7). The review from the source reports the second shape of the list as its finding F3 (a typed binding whose value is a let variable, as the head of a kept application).}
}

\subtree{
\title{RV2B-F2: a trigger gap in the class itself: only one of the two types has the K type at its head (wrong rejects of the published default and of the candidate)}

\p{Reported on the board at 03:08 UTC. With \code{\verb~|S.DP (x : True /\ True) : Prop := And.rec (fun _ _ => d intro = d intro) x~}, the type \code{\verb~|S.DP intro~} unfolds to \code{\verb~|d intro = d intro~}, \code{\verb~|S.DP h~} is stuck, and the two are convertible (the same head, a proof-irrelevant argument). Export \code{\verb~|rv2b-g1-eq-fn-app-SV-in~}: \code{\verb~|let f : Unit -> S.DP intro := fun u => axs; needs7 (Eq.rec 7 (f Unit.unit))~} with \code{\verb~|axs : S.DP h~}. Official: \strong{accept}: the declared type of \code{f} unfolds to an equation on which the K rule fires. Published build and candidate: \strong{reject}, exit status 1 on both launchers, exit 0 with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}; the mode is not entered (\code{\verb~|let.units=0~}) and the machine does not decline. The machine reduces the application away, types the reduct, finds a type whose weak head normal form does not have \code{Eq} at its head, and so makes no K comparison at all: the trigger of 2026-11-24 and every trigger of the package wait for a K comparison "whose type has the major inductive at its head".}

\p{The mirror after the let, \code{\verb~|rv2b-g1-eq-var-var-SD-out~}: \code{\verb~|P 7 := let x : S.DP h := axi; W x~} with \code{\verb~|axi : S.DP intro~} and \code{\verb~|W : forall (y : S.DP intro), P (Eq.rec 7 y)~}. Official: accept (after the let the major premise is \code{axi}). Both builds reject: the shape of finding MO2-F1, which the candidate repairs when the declared type is an equation and does not start for when it is stuck. In the first family, with a major premise that is not itself reducible: 24 wrong rejects inside the let (an application to one and to two arguments, an over-application, a projection of an application; a lambda and an axiom as the value) and 12 after it (the variable, a projection, an application of a projection), in both builds alike.}
}

\subtree{
\title{RV2B-F3: the projection of a constructor application as the major premise (wrong rejects of the published default and of the candidate)}

\p{Reported on the board at 03:08 UTC. \code{\verb~|rv2b-g5-none-var-projctor.ii-DF-in~}, without any let: \code{\verb~|needs7 (Eq.rec 7 (And.intro (d intro = d intro) True ax True.intro).1)~} with \code{\verb~|ax : 5 = d h~}. Official: \strong{accept}: \code{infer_proj} types the struct by the constructor's type, so the major premise has the type that is the first parameter, and the K rule fires. Both builds \strong{reject} (exit status 1, both launchers, exit 0 with the reference engine): the machine reduces the projection and types \code{ax}. A third reducible form of the major premise beside the beta-redex of MO2-F3 and the let term of MO2-F6, with the same root; 56 wrong rejects and 42 accepts of official rejects among the 308 exports of this shape in the families g1, g5 and g11, with a let variable, a redex binder or nothing around it. The review from the source has the same shape as its finding F6.}
}

\subtree{
\title{RV2B-F4: findings MO2-F3 and MO2-F6 with the let variable as the body (wrong rejects of the published default and of the candidate)}

\p{Reported on the board at 03:08 UTC. \code{\verb~|rv2b-g1-eq-var-constredex-DF-in~}: \code{\verb~|let x : d intro = d intro := ax; needs7 (Eq.rec 7 ((fun (z : Unit) => x) Unit.unit))~}, and \code{\verb~|rv2b-g1-eq-var-letbody-DF-in~} with \code{\verb~|(let w : Unit := Unit.unit; x)~} as the major premise. Official: \strong{accept} (the redex and the let term are typed through the body, the declared type of \code{x}). Both builds reject; the mode is not entered. The over-applied form \code{\verb~|(fun z => f) a b~} is finding MO2-F5, which the candidate repairs by a flag in the loop of APPLY; this form applies no let variable and sets no flag. 16 wrong rejects and 12 accepts of official rejects in the first family. The review from the source has it as its finding F5.}
}

\subtree{
\title{RV2B-F5: beyond 64 alias lets the mode is not started (a wrong reject of the published default and of the candidate)}

\p{Reported on the board at 03:23 UTC. \code{\verb~|let x0 : d h = 5 := ax; let x1 : d h = 5 := x0; ..; W x64 : P 7~} with \code{\verb~|ax : d intro = d intro~} and 65 lets (\code{\verb~|rv2b-p-aliaschain65-NF-out~}, 307 lines). Official: \strong{accept} (after the lets the major premise is \code{ax}). The published build rejects such a chain at any length (finding MO2-F1). The candidate: with 2, 5 and 15 lets its machine accepts; with 17 to 64 its machine declines (the mode's own judgments nest to 16 levels) and a plain run accepts through the reference engine; \strong{with 65 and more it rejects}, exit status 1 on both launchers, with \code{\verb~|let.units=0~}: the walk that looks for a let variable with two types behind the major premise stops, and the first run answers by the declared type. The twin with 64 lets is in the proposed rows. The review from the source reports another size limit of the same trigger (its finding F11, a frame beyond the near slots).}
}

\subtree{
\title{RV2B-F6: finding MO2-F1 at two other typing sites (wrong rejects of the published default and of the candidate)}

\p{Reported on the board at 03:23 UTC. The question that section 11 of the report of the package leaves open. A stuck projection keeps the let variable as its struct, and the official kernel types it through the declared type inside the let and through the value after it.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Structure eta in iota.} \code{\verb~|S.D2 (x : True /\ True) : Type := And.rec (fun _ _ => Nat × Nat) x~}; \code{\verb~|rv2b-g3-seta-proj-h.i-out~}: \code{\verb~|P 7 := let p : S.D2 h × Unit := axv; W p~} with \code{\verb~|axv : S.D2 intro × Unit~} and \code{\verb~|W : forall (y : S.D2 intro × Unit), P (Prod.rec (fun a b => 7) y.1)~}. Official: \strong{accept}: after the let the major premise \code{\verb~|axv.1~} has a pair type, is eta-expanded, and the recursor reduces. Both builds \strong{reject}: the machine types \code{\verb~|p.1~} by the declared type of \code{p}, a stuck type, and does not expand. The mirror (\code{\verb~|i.h~}) is rejected officially and accepted by both builds.}
\li{\strong{The unit-like rule.} \code{\verb~|S.U (n : Nat) : Type~} with one constructor without fields; \code{\verb~|rv2b-g3-unit-proj-h.i-out~}: \code{\verb~|let p : S.U (d h) × Unit := axu; W p~} with \code{\verb~|axu : S.U (d intro) × Unit~} against a goal that needs \code{\verb~|p.1~} convertible with an axiom of \code{\verb~|S.U 5~}. Official: accept after the let. Both builds reject; the mirror is accepted against the official verdict.}
\li{\strong{Proof irrelevance} on a stuck projection, on the let variable and on its application: no wrong reject in 64 exports. The machine accepts 34 that the official checker rejects (it compares proofs of types that it finds convertible where the official comparison of the two types fails); derivable statements.}
}

\p{The mode compares the scope states for the K rule only, so nothing of the package sees these; exit status 1 on both launchers of both builds, exit 0 with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. What held}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The class as the package states it.} With the major premise a let variable, its application to one or two arguments, an over-application, a projection, a projection of an application, an application of a projection, and an axiom as the value: 788 exports of the first family in the six flavours without a stuck type, all eleven positions, four K types. A plain run of the candidate gives the official verdict on every one (its machine alone declines 206); the published build differs on 117 of them (37 rejections of official accepts, 80 accepts of official rejects).}
\li{\strong{Chains} (896 exports): an alias, a lambda body, a partial application, a redex or a let term as the value term (the shapes of findings MO2-F2 and MO2-F4), a projection, three lets, a beta-redex binder as the outer or the inner link, in every firing pattern of the three types and with the inner let left and bound again. No deviation. The typed binding of section 7.3 of the report of the package (a let variable whose value is the variable of a redex, and a redex binder whose argument is a let variable) gave the official verdict in 280 exports.}
\li{\strong{The rules of the mode} (598 exports): nine lambda bodies, among them a redex, a let and a projection inside the body, two closures, a kept application with more and with fewer arguments than the lambda has binders, 70 lets before the pair; a marked variable that leaves the let under up to 4,000 applications of an axiom or as the last of up to 1,001 conjuncts whose others hold another let variable (the bounded search of section 4.4 of the report of the package); a let whose value is the K recursor application; two independent lets. No deviation; the machine alone declines 218.}
\li{\strong{Depth}: 2 to 70 lambda-body lets and 70 dummy lets around one give the official verdict; beyond 16 nested lets the machine declines, as the package says. The one deviation is finding RV2B-F5.}
\li{\strong{The stored type} (873 exports): a recursor application that INFER checked inside the let and that leaves it in the type of the body. No deviation of the candidate's own.}
\li{\strong{Sessions} (1,519 files): no deviation but in the shapes of RV2B-F1, RV2B-F4 and finding MO2-F3.}
\li{\strong{Not derivable} (1,064 inputs): rejected everywhere.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. The real units}

\p{\strong{The units.} The decline log of the published build names 86 units of the class on the eight corpora; they are 75 distinct declarations (Std 1, con-leche 1, navier-stokes-euler 16, cslib 5 more, Mathlib 52 more). Each was cut with \src{bench/workloads/slice-export.py} into a self-contained export: the declaration, the statements of the theorems it uses, the definitions with their values (0.4 to 26 MB, 389 MB together). The official checker accepts all 75.}

\p{\strong{Unchanged.} A plain run of the candidate accepts all 75. Its machine alone decides 69 (66 of them in the mode, one with a K comparison through a let variable with two types) and declines 6: the six units that the report of the package names. The base's machine declines 73 and decides 2 by itself: alone in an export, without the session history of its corpus, these two units do not set the flag.}

\p{\strong{Structural mutants} (1,311, written by a tool that re-encodes the slice): every let of the declaration turned into a beta-redex, and one let at a time (the official checker rejects 125 of these 221: the body needs the value of the variable); a beta-redex turned into a let; a let inlined; the declared type of a let wrapped in a let term; the value of a let turned into an identity redex over the declared type, and into a let term; the major premise of every application of \code{Eq.rec}, \code{Eq.ndrec}, \code{Eq.mpr}, \code{Eq.mp} and \code{cast} turned into an identity redex typed by the equation of the application, into a let term, and put under an axiom \code{\verb~|RV.hide : a = b -> a = b~}, so that the recursor reduces by the K rule or not at all. \strong{A plain run of the candidate gives the official verdict on all 1,309 that have one}, and so does the published build. The candidate's machine alone decides every mutant of the 69 units and declines every mutant of the six; no mutation moved a unit from one group to the other. The real K comparisons do not need the major premise to reduce: with every such major premise behind the axiom (69 of the 75 units have one), every mutant is still accepted, officially and by the candidate.}

\p{\strong{Grafts} (654): a hostile K site of the first family (nine shapes of the major premise, four flavours, three positions) around the whole value, the body of a let or the value of a let of the real declaration, so that the hostile let is bound and left among the real ones. The official checker accepts 403 and rejects 251, each as the model predicts. With the major premise a let variable, an application, an over-application, a projection or an application with two arguments: the candidate gives the official verdict on every graft; its machine alone declines 91 of the 654 (the grafts into the six units, and those in which the readings differ and both states are met). With a reducible major premise: 48 wrong rejects and 4 accepts of official rejects, 46 of the 52 its own. Ten of the 46 have the plain identity redex of finding MO2-F3: alone in an export the published build rejects that shape too, but inside a real unit of the class its machine declines the unit for the unit's own let applications, and the reference engine gives the official verdict. \strong{So each of the units that the package moves from the reference engine to the machine is, from then on, open to the unrepaired redex class}, where it was not before.}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. The schedule}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Files with several declarations}: the 367 files of the session family and the 1,152 of the warm family, each under \code{\verb~|--threads 1~}, \code{4} and \code{8} on the executable and under 1 and 8 workers on the JVM launcher, beside the plain run: one verdict per file in all six configurations, on all 1,519.}
\li{\strong{The two launchers}: 3,039 single-declaration exports of the families g1, g2, g3, g7 and g8 through the JVM launcher, in a plain run and for the machine alone: the same exit status as the executable on every one.}
\li{\strong{Real mutants}: a quarter of them (492, every fourth in name order: 331 structural mutants and 161 grafts; these exports have hundreds to thousands of declarations around the mutated one) under the same six configurations: one verdict per file on all 492.}
\li{\strong{The order of the declarations} decides one verdict of the \em{published} build: with the identity redex of finding MO2-F3 over a let variable, two declarations "inside, then after" are accepted and "after, then inside" are rejected, where the official checker accepts both and each declaration alone is rejected. The candidate rejects both orders. This is the dependence on the history of a session that the project knows ([the insight](ins-RPOG)); it does not move with the number of workers: six runs of each of these files under 1, 4 and 8 workers of the published executable give one verdict per file.}
\li{\strong{The path depends on the schedule, the verdict does not.} The real unit \code{FirstOrder.Language.DirectLimit.equiv_lift}, alone in its export, in six runs per configuration: with one worker the candidate's machine accepts it in the first run every time; with 4 and 8 workers it accepts it in the first run in some runs and in the mode in others (\code{\verb~|let.units~} 0 or 1), and the base's machine accepts or declines (exit status 0 or 2 with \code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~}). Whether the first run flags the application of a let variable depends on what the session has evaluated before. On this unit both paths accept. No generated file was found on which the two paths give different verdicts; the session and warm families were written for that and did not find one.}
}

}

\subtree{
\title{8. Declines of the machine alone}

\p{The aim of the package is that the machine decides. On the inputs of this review the candidate's machine alone declines 1,804 (the base's 4,911). Where the two readings of the type agree, which is the case of every K comparison of the real corpora that the package counted, the candidate's machine declines: the six real units and all their structural mutants (101 inputs); a chain of 17 or more alias or lambda-body lets (98 generated inputs, in every flavour); the over-application of a redex that returns a let variable and a let term in the head position of an application, in every flavour (the rule of finding MO2-F5). Where the readings differ and both scope states are met in one declaration it declines by design: the rows carry the note \code{let-scope} in the proposed fragment.}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Proposed rows}

\p{163 exports chosen for coverage, with their official verdict files and a manifest fragment in the format of the hostile suite, are handed over with the review; they are not rows of the suite. \strong{123 rows} (70 accept, 53 reject) on which a plain run of the candidate gives the official verdict, 29 of them with the note \code{let-scope} (its machine alone declines): positions and K types that the rows of the package do not have, the chains, the lambda bodies, the buried marked variable, the long chains, the sessions, the typed bindings, the other typing sites where the machine is right, and 12 rows that are not derivable (strict). \strong{40 rows} on which it does not: 29 official accepts that a plain run rejects (10 of them only the candidate: RV2B-F1; the others shared with the published build: RV2B-F2 to F6 and the two known shapes with a let variable) and 11 accepts of official rejects (derivable; 5 of them only the candidate). Under \src{scripts/gate-hostile.py} in the default configuration: the candidate agrees on all 123 and has 29 rejections under the mark and 11 tolerated accepts on the 40; the published build has 26 wrong rejects and 23 wrong accepts on the 123 (the rows are strict) and 19 rejections under the mark on the 40; with the reference engine both builds agree on all 163.}
}

\subtree{
\title{10. What this review did not cover}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The x86-64 architecture}: everything ran on the aarch64 development host.}
\li{\strong{Whole corpora}: no corpus was run; the real units were run alone in their exports, without the session history of their corpus. The report of the package has the corpus runs.}
\li{\strong{Three quarters of the real mutants under the schedule} and on the JVM launcher (section 7 has one quarter).}
\li{\strong{Mutants that change what a real K comparison decides.} No structural mutation of a real unit made a real K comparison answer differently under the two readings: the bad mutants among them are rejected for the missing value of a let variable, and the K-decisive bad inputs on real units are the grafts, whose K site is the generator's.}
\li{\strong{K types in mutual and nested blocks, and other K types than equalities} with indices of other sorts than \code{Nat} (one universe-polymorphic K type on \code{Nat}; the type-level triple of the package's rows was not repeated).}
\li{\strong{Eta expansion and structure eta in the comparison} (not in iota) as typing sites, and string and numeral literals as major premises.}
\li{\strong{The options of the package} other than \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.letexact=off~} on the finding exports; every declaration in the mode (\code{\verb~|letexact=always~}) was not run.}
\li{\strong{Why}: this review names shapes and verdicts. The causes given in section 4 are read off the counters, the messages and the comparison of builds, not off the source.}
}
}
